% ============================================================================
% cloud-signal — for cirrus and stratus clouds, the INDIVIDUAL weather
% signal(s) an already-cited NWS sentence lists for each, decoded one-per-row
% (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `cloud-type.adj` (`cloud_type(cloud,
% weather_indication)`, each of three common cloud types and the single
% combined weather indication its presence signals). Two of that table's own
% three quoted NWS sentences each list MORE than one signal, joined by an
% exhaustive "or" list -- a fact the single `weather_indication` atom folded
% into one compound label, with no room to decode each listed item as its
% own queryable fact:
%
%   cirrus (two alternatives joined by "or", in the parent table's own
%   `source` field):
%     "They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or
%      upper-level jet streak."
%
%   stratus (three alternatives, an "or"/"or" chain, quoted in the parent
%   table's own header):
%     "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud
%      cover which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light
%      precipitation or drizzle."
%     (the page has a NON-BREAKING SPACE, U+00A0, after "precipitation-free",
%      which this comment cannot show; the table shipped an ordinary space there
%      until 2026-09-15, a string the page never wrote. The row's `source` now
%      carries the page's character.)
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? cloud_signal(stratus, $Signal)
%       % precipitation_free, light_precipitation, AND drizzle (three answers)
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `cloud_signal(cloud, signal)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the
% engine returns each signal WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on a cloud whose own cited span names only ONE signal
% with no listed alternative. `cumulonimbus` is deliberately NOT a row here:
% its own already-quoted span ("a mature, deep cumulonimbus cloud, i.e., a
% thunderstorm producing heavy rain") names exactly one signal -- no
% enumerated "or"/list of alternatives to decode -- so this sibling is
% HONESTLY NARROWER than its parent, grounding exactly the two cloud types
% whose cited spans list more than one signal and abstaining on the third,
% or any other word, rather than inventing one. The query also runs
% BACKWARD -- bind a signal and recall which cloud type indicates it:
%
%     ? cloud_signal($Cloud, upper_level_jet_streak)
%       % cirrus
%
% Provenance: the SAME two NWS sentences `cloud-type.adj` cites, from the NWS
% Louisville "Cloud Classification" page, decoding each listed signal as its
% own row. EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN CLOUD'S SENTENCE AS ITS `source`
% (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). This paragraph used to say the spans were
% reproduced "byte-for-byte" with "no new WebFetch", and that an ADJ `table`
% carries ONE provenance envelope -- so it held the cirrus value sentence as
% the envelope, which made it the primary source of the three stratus answers
% too. Neither claim survives a raw fetch: the language has per-row blocks,
% and the stratus sentence was not byte-for-byte (see the U+00A0 note above).
% Each sentence names both its cloud and its signals, so each warrants its
% rows. The envelope is now the page's framing sentence, "Clouds are
% classified according to their height above and appearance (texture) from
% the ground.", which names no cloud and no signal. Measured 2026-09-15: the
% cirrus span, the stratus sentence (in the page's own form) and the envelope
% each occur exactly once. `trust authoritative` -- NWS Louisville forecast
% office, a primary U.S. government science-education source.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table cloud_signal {
    columns cloud, signal

    row (cirrus, approaching_warm_front) {
        source "Cirrus clouds are wispy, feathery, and composed entirely of ice crystals. They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or upper-level jet streak."
    }
    row (cirrus, upper_level_jet_streak) {
        source "Cirrus clouds are wispy, feathery, and composed entirely of ice crystals. They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or upper-level jet streak."
    }
    row (stratus, precipitation_free) {
        % U+00A0 after "precipitation-free", as on the page.
        source "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud cover which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light precipitation or drizzle."
    }
    row (stratus, light_precipitation) {
        % U+00A0 after "precipitation-free", as on the page.
        source "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud cover which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light precipitation or drizzle."
    }
    row (stratus, drizzle) {
        % U+00A0 after "precipitation-free", as on the page.
        source "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud cover which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light precipitation or drizzle."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE CIRRUS VALUE SENTENCE, and so the primary source of the stratus answers.
    % Each row now carries its own cloud's sentence; this framing
    % sentence names no cloud and no weather signal.
    source "Clouds are classified according to their height above and appearance (texture) from the ground."
    locator "https://www.weather.gov/lmk/cloud_classification"
    trust authoritative
}
